\begin{abstract}
We present a compact, hardware-matched variational quantum circuit (VQC) for the Max-Cut problem and illustrate it on a five-qubit instance validated on an ion-trap quantum computer in the accompanying source document. The circuit comprises two layers of parameterized single-qubit rotations separated by a fixed entangling layer and is trained with a classical optimizer using the parameter-shift rule. On the documented five-node graph, the ion-trap experiment concentrated roughly 95\% of the measurement probability on the theoretically optimal cut assignments. Using a noiseless state-vector simulator, our implementation reaches the exact optimum cut value and concentrates essentially all probability on the same four optimal bit strings. We release a minimal, dependency-light reference implementation to facilitate replication and extension.
\end{abstract}

\section{Background and Motivation}
A wide range of resource-allocation tasks in wireless networks---including spectrum partitioning, user clustering, base-station switching, and backhaul routing---can be abstracted as graph partitioning problems such as Max-Cut. Given the NP-hardness of Max-Cut, scalable exact solvers are unlikely, and approximate classical algorithms may struggle on highly structured or weighted instances.

Variational quantum algorithms (VQAs) aim to mitigate this challenge by encoding the objective into a cost Hamiltonian and using a shallow, parameterized quantum circuit whose parameters are trained by a classical optimizer. The source document follows this approach on an ion-trap device, reporting strong concentration of probability mass on optimal cut assignments for a five-node graph, thereby validating the feasibility of the approach on real hardware.

\section{Problem Formulation}
For an undirected graph $G=(V,E)$ with unit weights, the Max-Cut objective can be written as the expectation value of the Ising Hamiltonian
\begin{equation}
    H_C \;=\; \sum_{(i,j)\in E} \frac{1 - Z_i Z_j}{2},
\end{equation}
where $Z_i$ is the Pauli-$Z$ operator on qubit $i$. Maximizing $\langle H_C \rangle$ is equivalent to minimizing the loss $-\langle H_C \rangle$.

\paragraph{Five-node instance.}
We adopt the same instance described in the source document:
\begin{equation}
    V = \{0,1,2,3,4\}, \qquad
    E = \{(0, 1), (0, 2), (0, 4), (1, 2), (2, 3), (3, 4)\}.
\end{equation}
Its maximum cut value is $5$, attained by exactly four assignments (written as $|q_4 q_3 q_2 q_1 q_0\rangle$):
\begin{equation*}
    10100,\; 10110,\; 01001,\; 01011.
\end{equation*}
In the source, bit strings are listed right-to-left; the set above is the same up to reversal.

\section{Hardware-Matched Variational Circuit}
We use a shallow \emph{hardware-matched} VQC that mirrors constraints of ion-trap devices: a \textit{front} layer of single-qubit rotations, a fixed \textit{entangling} layer that is independent of the problem graph, and a \textit{back} layer of single-qubit rotations,
\begin{equation}
    \Big(\prod_i R_y(\theta_i)\, R_z(\phi_i)\Big)\;\; \mathcal{E}_{\mathrm{ring}}\;\; \Big(\prod_i R_y(\theta'_i)\, R_z(\phi'_i)\Big),
\end{equation}
where $\mathcal{E}_{\mathrm{ring}}$ is a ring of controlled-$Z$ gates. Parameters are updated via gradient descent with gradients estimated using the parameter-shift rule. The cost $-\langle H_C \rangle$ is evaluated from outcome probabilities only (since $Z_i Z_j$ is diagonal), which reduces sampling overhead on real hardware.

\section{Results}
We implemented a dependency-light state-vector simulator (NumPy only) and optimized $20$ parameters for $180$ iterations with a fixed learning rate of $0.15$. Figure~\ref{fig:energy} shows the objective converging rapidly to the optimum. The final state concentrates probability almost entirely on the four optimal strings, with total mass $100.0\%$; in the noiseless setting, the achieved cut value equals the theoretical maximum of $5$ for this instance.

