\section{Discussion}
Our ansatz matches the qualitative structure reported in the ion-trap study: two parameterized single-qubit layers sandwich a fixed entangling layer; a quantum--classical loop updates parameters via the parameter-shift rule; and the five-qubit Max-Cut instance admits four optimal assignments with cut value $5$. The simulation provides a noiseless upper bound for hardware performance and a convenient sandbox to compare different optimizers, entangling patterns, and initialization strategies. Furthermore, the explicit separation between a problem-independent entangler and problem-dependent cost can be useful for co-design with specific chain and connectivity constraints on trapped-ion systems.

\section{Reproducibility}
The single-file implementation \texttt{simulation.py} accompanying this paper:
\begin{itemize}
  \item Encodes the instance $E$ above;
  \item Implements the VQC and the parameter-shift optimizer;
  \item Saves the energy trace (\texttt{energy.png}) and the final-state spectrum (\texttt{state\_probs.png});
  \item Writes a machine-readable summary to \texttt{results.json}.
\end{itemize}
Running \texttt{python simulation.py} reproduces the figures in this paper.

\section{Conclusion}
By aligning a simple VQC with hardware constraints and optimizing it classically, we obtain high-quality solutions to Max-Cut on a five-qubit instance. These results complement the ion-trap evaluation reported in the source document and provide a minimal, fully reproducible simulator for further study and benchmarking.

